# The committed R diagnostic records are checked byte for byte against a SHA-256 manifest on
# every read (`benchmarks/drtmle_r_compare.py`'s `_verify`), and that check is the whole reason
# they are in the tree: the R toolchain is ~25 minutes to rebuild and CRAN is not always
# reachable, so `docs/drtmle/r-differential.md`'s comparison and F4's verification have to
# reproduce with no R installed.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` -- the Windows default -- rewrites those files' line endings on
# checkout, which changes their bytes and so their digest. The manifest then fails against the
# record it describes, and `pytest -m "not slow"` cannot pass on a default Windows clone:
# measured here as 39 errors and 1 failure in `tests/unit/test_drtmle_r_compare.py`, with the
# LF-only digest matching the manifest exactly and the CRLF one not.
#
# `-text` disables that conversion, so these files are checked out with the bytes they were
# committed with, on every platform. This is not a regeneration -- CLAUDE.md forbids that, and
# every trace already taken is against these bytes -- it is what keeps the checkout equal to them.
benchmarks/fixtures/r-trace-*/** -text
benchmarks/fixtures/r-ladder-*/** -text
